WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.459
Lesson Six brings the earlier force work and the motion work together.

2
00:00:05.459 --> 00:00:10.918
F equals m a says the acceleration depends on the resultant force and the mass.

3
00:00:10.918 --> 00:00:16.377
If the resultant force is larger while the mass stays fixed, the acceleration gets larger.

4
00:00:16.377 --> 00:00:21.836
If the mass is larger while the same resultant force acts, the acceleration gets smaller.

5
00:00:21.836 --> 00:00:27.295
That is why the same push does not change every object in the same way.

6
00:00:27.295 --> 00:00:32.755
The equation is really a statement about response.

7
00:00:32.755 --> 00:00:38.214
Keep this takeaway in view: F = ma is about how strongly motion changes when a certain resultant force acts on a certain mass.

8
00:00:38.214 --> 00:00:43.673
More resultant force means more acceleration.

9
00:00:43.673 --> 00:00:49.132
If mass stays fixed, a bigger overall force changes the motion more strongly.

10
00:00:49.132 --> 00:00:54.591
More mass means less acceleration.

11
00:00:54.591 --> 00:01:00.050
If force stays fixed, more mass makes the motion harder to change.

12
00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:05.632
The equation can be written in several useful forms, but the meaning stays the same.

13
00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:11.215
Force equals mass times acceleration.

14
00:01:11.215 --> 00:01:16.798
If you need acceleration, rearrange to a equals F over m.

15
00:01:16.798 --> 00:01:22.380
If you need force, multiply mass by acceleration.

16
00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:27.962
But do not forget the most important instruction: the force in the equation is the resultant force, the net force left after combining all the forces from the previous lesson.

17
00:01:27.962 --> 00:01:33.545
That link between lessons is essential.

18
00:01:33.545 --> 00:01:39.128
Keep this takeaway in view: The equation is simple only when the force value is already the resultant force value.

19
00:01:39.128 --> 00:01:44.710
If force is given: Check first that it is the resultant force rather than an uncombined list of forces.

20
00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:50.292
If mass is given: Keep it in kilograms so the acceleration unit stays consistent.

21
00:01:50.292 --> 00:01:55.875
F = ma.

22
00:01:55.875 --> 00:02:01.458
a = F / m.

23
00:02:01.458 --> 00:02:07.040
Use the resultant force after combining all pushes and pulls.

24
00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:12.432
Imagine pushing a light trolley and a heavy trolley with the same resultant force.

25
00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:17.824
The light trolley speeds up more because less mass is being asked to change its motion.

26
00:02:17.824 --> 00:02:23.216
The heavy trolley speeds up less because more mass resists that same change.

27
00:02:23.216 --> 00:02:28.608
This is the physical meaning behind the division in a equals F over m.

28
00:02:28.608 --> 00:02:34.000
Mass is not just a number in the denominator.

29
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.392
It controls how responsive the object is to a given resultant force.

30
00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:44.784
Keep this takeaway in view: The same push does not guarantee the same acceleration.

31
00:02:44.784 --> 00:02:50.176
The mass decides how strongly the object responds.

32
00:02:50.176 --> 00:02:55.568
Light cart: Less mass means the same force can change the motion more easily.

33
00:02:55.568 --> 00:03:00.960
Heavy cart: More mass means the same force produces a smaller acceleration.

34
00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.151
The concept word behind the equation is inertia.

35
00:03:06.151 --> 00:03:11.342
Inertia means resistance to changing motion.

36
00:03:11.342 --> 00:03:16.532
More mass means more inertia.

37
00:03:16.532 --> 00:03:21.723
That is why a loaded shopping cart feels much harder to set moving or to stop quickly than an empty one.

38
00:03:21.723 --> 00:03:26.914
The same idea also explains why seatbelts matter.

39
00:03:26.914 --> 00:03:32.105
When a car changes motion suddenly, your body tends to continue its previous motion unless another force acts.

40
00:03:32.105 --> 00:03:37.296
Inertia is the conceptual bridge between everyday experience and F equals m a.

41
00:03:37.296 --> 00:03:42.487
Keep this takeaway in view: Inertia gives a physical reason for why larger mass leads to smaller acceleration under the same resultant force.

42
00:03:42.487 --> 00:03:47.677
Inertia means resistance to change.

43
00:03:47.677 --> 00:03:52.868
It describes how strongly an object resists changes in its motion.

44
00:03:52.868 --> 00:03:58.059
More mass means more inertia.

45
00:03:58.059 --> 00:04:03.250
Heavier objects are harder to speed up, slow down, or redirect.

46
00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:09.289
Take a trolley with a 12 newton resultant force and a mass of 3 kilograms.

47
00:04:09.289 --> 00:04:15.328
Use a equals F over m.

48
00:04:15.328 --> 00:04:21.367
Twelve divided by three gives 4 metres per second squared.

49
00:04:21.367 --> 00:04:27.406
Now keep the force the same but double the mass to 6 kilograms.

50
00:04:27.406 --> 00:04:33.445
The new acceleration becomes 12 divided by 6, which is 2 metres per second squared.

51
00:04:33.445 --> 00:04:39.485
That comparison is important because it shows the inverse relationship directly: doubling the mass halves the acceleration when the force stays the same.

52
00:04:39.485 --> 00:04:45.524
Keep this takeaway in view: Numerical comparison makes the equation feel physical: same force, bigger mass, smaller acceleration.

53
00:04:45.524 --> 00:04:51.563
First trolley means 4 m/s^2.

54
00:04:51.563 --> 00:04:57.602
12 N divided by 3 kg gives the original acceleration.

55
00:04:57.602 --> 00:05:03.641
Mass doubled means 2 m/s^2.

56
00:05:03.641 --> 00:05:09.680
12 N divided by 6 kg gives the new smaller acceleration.

57
00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:14.218
One last safeguard matters here.

58
00:05:14.218 --> 00:05:18.755
If multiple forces act on the object, you cannot choose one of them at random and put it into F equals m a.

59
00:05:18.755 --> 00:05:23.293
You must first combine them to find the resultant force.

60
00:05:23.293 --> 00:05:27.831
Only that net leftover force tells you how the motion will change.

61
00:05:27.831 --> 00:05:32.368
So lesson five and lesson six are really a chain: first find the resultant force, then use it with the mass to find the acceleration.

62
00:05:32.368 --> 00:05:36.906
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson five gives you the force to use.

63
00:05:36.906 --> 00:05:41.444
Lesson six tells you what that force does to the motion.

64
00:05:41.444 --> 00:05:45.982
Step before the equation: Combine all the forces to get the resultant force.

65
00:05:45.982 --> 00:05:50.519
Why: Only the net overall force controls the actual change in motion.

66
00:05:50.519 --> 00:05:55.057
Many forces acting?

67
00:05:55.057 --> 00:05:59.595
Combine them first.

68
00:05:59.595 --> 00:06:04.132
Need acceleration?

69
00:06:04.132 --> 00:06:08.670
Use a = F / m.

70
00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:12.693
Check the relationship quickly.

71
00:06:12.693 --> 00:06:16.716
Which force belongs in F equals m a?

72
00:06:16.716 --> 00:06:20.739
The resultant force.

73
00:06:20.739 --> 00:06:24.762
If the same force acts on two objects but one object has more mass, which one has the smaller acceleration?

74
00:06:24.762 --> 00:06:28.785
The more massive object.

75
00:06:28.785 --> 00:06:32.808
What word names resistance to motion change?

76
00:06:32.808 --> 00:06:36.832
Inertia.

77
00:06:36.832 --> 00:06:40.855
Those three answers form the conceptual backbone of this lesson.

78
00:06:40.855 --> 00:06:44.878
Keep this takeaway in view: The example only asks you to express mathematically what these three relationships already say physically.

79
00:06:44.878 --> 00:06:48.901
Force used means resultant force.

80
00:06:48.901 --> 00:06:52.924
Use the net force after combining all pushes and pulls.

81
00:06:52.924 --> 00:06:56.947
Same force, more mass means smaller acceleration.

82
00:06:56.947 --> 00:07:00.970
More mass means the motion changes less for the same force.

83
00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:07.305
You are ready for the worked example now.

84
00:07:07.305 --> 00:07:13.641
A 12 newton resultant force acts on a 3 kilogram trolley.

85
00:07:13.641 --> 00:07:19.976
Use a equals F over m because the question gives force and mass and asks for acceleration.

86
00:07:19.976 --> 00:07:26.312
Twelve divided by three gives 4 metres per second squared.

87
00:07:26.312 --> 00:07:32.647
Then imagine the mass doubling to 6 kilograms while the force stays at 12 newtons.

88
00:07:32.647 --> 00:07:38.982
The new acceleration becomes 12 divided by 6, so 2 metres per second squared.

89
00:07:38.982 --> 00:07:45.318
That shows the full lesson pattern: same force, larger mass, smaller acceleration.

90
00:07:45.318 --> 00:07:51.653
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: use the resultant force, divide by the mass, and interpret the response physically.

91
00:07:51.653 --> 00:07:57.988
Step 1: Make sure the force given is the resultant force.

92
00:07:57.988 --> 00:08:04.324
Step 2: Use a = F / m to calculate the acceleration.

93
00:08:04.324 --> 00:08:10.659
a = F / m = 12 / 3 = 4 m/s^2.

94
00:08:10.659 --> 00:08:16.995
if m doubles to 6 kg, a = 12 / 6 = 2 m/s^2.

95
00:08:16.995 --> 00:08:23.330
The force used here is the resultant force, not one uncombined force from a list.
